# TT.EVENT_INTERFACE.exec_event_type_actions -- the corpus's one dynamic-SQL site (S-EVENT-038,
# migration/spec/DYNAMIC-SQL-TRACE.md) exercised END-TO-END on both engines: pilot event types
# (956301..956308, source 1, outside is_esc_supp_active's 10002/10005/10007/10009 set) are mapped
# through tt.event_type_action to (a) master-data action 1 (procedure_name
# tt.escalation_interface.escalate_os), (b) pilot actions naming escalate_customer, nop and
# escalate_isp_outage_to_os, (c) a pilot action naming a REAL single-NUMBER-argument procedure
# that is NOT on the translation's TT.ESCALATION_INTERFACE allowlist
# (tt.escalation_interface.clear_customer_escalation), (d) master-data action 2 (DBPROP key 139,
# no procedure_name) for the OSAG_DBPROP lookup path, (e) action 2 under an active service
# window with disable_conn_escalation (S-EVENT-039/041, the 60010 "escalation suppressed" event),
# plus an unmapped type, an inactive host (S-ESC-006 via dispatch), a CLOSED ticket (S-ESC-009:
# addevent_new's ticket_closed swallowed by exec_event_type_actions' own WHEN OTHERS) and a
# nonexistent event id (S-EVENT-035, P0002 unhandled on this direct call path).
#
# known.diff -- ONE documented, deliberate difference (S-EVENT-038 allowlist, DYNAMIC-SQL-TRACE.md
# step 2, threats T-02-44/45): Oracle EXECUTEs whatever tt.event_action names -- here
# clear_customer_escalation(956105) runs and resets ticket 956105 from escalation status 20 to 0,
# step 05 is 'ok'; the translation refuses with a labelled RAISE EXCEPTION (P0001) placed OUTSIDE
# the swallowing block on purpose, so step 05 records P0001 and ticket 956105 keeps 20.
#
# Observed live and NOT a difference (2026-10-21): the closed ticket 956109. escalate_os's UPDATE
# (status := 10) followed by addevent_new's ticket_closed error is undone on BOTH engines when
# exec_event_type_actions' WHEN OTHERS swallows it -- Oracle rolls back the failing dynamic CALL
# statement as a whole, PL/pgSQL rolls back the EXCEPTION block's subtransaction. The
# transaction-scope delta migration/spec/tt.escalation_interface.md records for XCUT-05 therefore
# does not exist on the dynamic-dispatch path (it does on a static PL/SQL call, see
# tt__escalation_interface__escalate).
#
# Observed live: step 06 (DBPROP action 2 with a host) needed migration/test/schema-fixes/
# 28_dbprop_mviews_fixes.sql -- mss.v_host_key_value and its two materialized views were missing
# from the template (42P01 on PostgreSQL, SCHEMA-FIXES.md fix #12); with the view both engines
# return NULL (no DBPROP value for the fixture host) and execute nothing.
#
# ORA_USER=system: the fixtures insert into stop.company, mss.host, mss.service, mss.host2service
# and TT's reference tables; TT holds only SELECT on the STOP/MSS tables. Everything is rolled
# back (COMMITS=no); only Oracle's autonomous plog rows survive.
ORA_USER=system
COMMITS=no
COMPARE_MSG=no
DESCRIPTION=exec_event_type_actions dispatch to escalate_os / escalate_customer / nop / escalate_isp_outage_to_os, off-allowlist procedure, DBPROP action, service-window suppression (60010), inactive host, closed ticket, nonexistent event
